    "grp_igbp": {
      "igbp": "EBF",
      "igbp_comment": "Secondary Forest: Encompassing 70 percent of the region, mature secondary-growth forests, approximately 40 years old, paint a vibrant picture. These forests stand out with some emergent trees, including Ceiba spp., Inga spp., and Hura crepitans, soaring to around 40 meters in height. The landscape features frequent tree-fall gaps, predominantly occupied by pioneer trees like Cecropia spp. and other low wood density species, alongside numerous juvenile individuals with a diameter at breast height (DAP) of less than 10 cm. Lianas weave through the environment, often reaching a diameter (dbh) of more than 10 cm. Ten percent of the region hosts temporary swamp forests. Nestled in low-lying areas, these ecosystems experience periodic standing water derived from rain, persisting for over six months annually. During dry months, the highly organic soil retains a consistently soggy state."
    },
